(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-respiratory-infections-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Respiratory Infections and Vaccines Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 30-year-old man with no medical conditions has community-acquired pneumonia confirmed on chest radiograph and is stable for outpatient care. Molecular testing is positive for a respiratory virus, and he can be seen again in 2 days. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Add azithromycin for atypicals","No antibiotic, close follow-up","Amoxicillin despite the result","Admission for ceftriaxone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A viral-positive outpatient without comorbidities may avoid empiric antibiotics when close follow-up is reliable. His positive molecular test, absent comorbidities and arranged review in 2 days meet those conditions; antibiotics treat a bacterial syndrome, not a positive viral test. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 47, 126"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure is hospitalized on day 5 of influenza, confirmed by molecular testing, with hypoxemia. Which antiviral is most appropriate?","choices":["Amantadine","Oral or enteric oseltamivir","Inhaled zanamivir","Single-dose baloxavir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized patients generally receive oral or enteric oseltamivir, which is preferred for hospitalized, severe and high-risk patients and is started even after 48 hours. His age, lung and heart disease and admission all point to it, with renal dose adjustment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 97, 98, 108, 109"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with ventilator-associated pneumonia has had an adequate response to culture-directed antibiotics. What is the usual total duration of therapy?","choices":["3 days","5 days","7 days","14 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia, a 7-day course is usual when the response is adequate, adjusted to clinical response, with prompt de-escalation as cultures clarify the diagnosis. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 46, 49"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with community-acquired pneumonia is alert and oriented. Her blood urea nitrogen is 17 mg/dL, respiratory rate 34/min and blood pressure 132/56 mm Hg. What is her CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score?","choices":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Confusion 0 + urea 0 (17 is not above 20) + respiratory rate 1 (34 is 30 or more) + blood pressure 1 (a diastolic of 56 is 60 or lower, even with a normal systolic) + age 1 (81) = 3. That is high severity, with a risk of death of 15 to 40%: hospital and urgent senior review. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with alcohol use disorder has 4 weeks of fevers, sweats, fatigue and a cough with foul-smelling purulent sputum. A chest radiograph shows a cavity with an air-fluid level. Which initial therapy is most appropriate?","choices":["Immediate surgical resection","Routine chest physiotherapy","Intravenous ampicillin-sulbactam","Clindamycin as first choice"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A cavity with an air-fluid level after weeks of fever and foul sputum is a lung abscess, which usually begins with antibiotics. A beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor is the usual first choice, such as intravenous ampicillin-sulbactam followed by oral amoxicillin-clavulanate, often for several weeks. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 56"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with influenza was improving on day 4, but on day 6 he develops a new fever, a cough productive of purulent sputum and a lobar infiltrate on chest radiograph. Which organisms most likely cause this complication?","choices":["Pneumocystis or cytomegalovirus","Pneumococcus or staphylococcus","Mycoplasma or Chlamydia psittaci","Histoplasma or Cryptococcus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Influenza pneumonia may be primary influenza pneumonia or a secondary bacterial pneumonia, often a pneumococcal or staphylococcal co-infection. Improvement followed by worsening, purulent sputum and a lobar infiltrate point to the secondary bacterial pneumonia, which is treated with antibiotics. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia is alert, with a blood urea nitrogen of 15 mg/dL, a respiratory rate of 22/min and a blood pressure of 134/82 mm Hg. His chronic heart failure has become unstable. Where should he be treated?","choices":["Home with oral antibiotics","Critical care unit","Home without antibiotics","Hospital ward admission"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His severity total is 1: confusion 0 (alert), urea 0 (15 is not above 20), respiratory rate 0 (22), blood pressure 0 and age 1 (66). A total of 0 to 1 is low severity, with a risk of death under 3%, but the pathway still admits for an unstable comorbidity or a social reason, and his heart failure is unstable. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31, 47"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has coronavirus disease 2019 with fever, cough and mild shortness of breath. Her oxygen saturation is 96% on room air and her respiratory rate is 20/min, and a chest radiograph shows a patchy infiltrate. Which severity category fits?","choices":["Mild illness","Moderate illness","Severe illness","Critical illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Moderate illness has symptoms with a positive chest radiograph and an oxygen saturation of at least 94% on room air. Her patchy infiltrate makes her more than mild, and her saturation of 96% with a normal respiratory rate keeps her below severe. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is starting doxycycline for Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection. She asks what to expect and how to avoid getting it again. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["Return if the cough lasts past day 3","The cough may persist for a while","A vaccine can prevent another episode","Amoxicillin is a good backup drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Counseling for Mycoplasma infection explains that the cough may persist, and that she should return for shortness of breath or deterioration. No vaccine is available, so avoiding another episode depends on general measures, not immunization. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and diabetes has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. She has had a severe reaction to fluoroquinolone antibiotics as a class. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Azithromycin as a single drug","Cefuroxime plus azithromycin","Moxifloxacin, with QT review","Doxycycline as a single drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her lung disease and diabetes are listed comorbidities, calling for amoxicillin-clavulanate, cefpodoxime or cefuroxime plus azithromycin or doxycycline, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone. Her reaction to the fluoroquinolone class takes that option off the table, so the beta-lactam combination is used. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 36-year-old pet-shop employee has had a week of fever, severe headache, photophobia and a nonproductive cough. His pulse is 72/min with a temperature of 103°F, his spleen is palpable, and macular rashes on his face resemble the rose spots of typhoid fever. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute histoplasmosis","Influenza","Psittacosis","Mycoplasma infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Psittacosis is an occupational infection of pet-shop and zoo employees and poultry workers, caught from birds such as parakeets. Severe headache, photophobia, a dry cough, relative bradycardia, splenomegaly and Horder spots (facial macular rashes like typhoid rose spots) complete the picture. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 83, 75, 76, 89, 99"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman develops ventilator-associated pneumonia on day 5 of mechanical ventilation. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is common in her unit. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Cefepime plus vancomycin","Cefepime as a single drug","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Moxifloxacin plus linezolid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Local methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus prevalence adds vancomycin or linezolid to an antipseudomonal beta-lactam such as cefepime. Cultures are obtained, and therapy is reassessed daily and de-escalated. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 46, 42, 48"},{"q":"A healthy 29-year-old woman has acute histoplasmosis confirmed by urine antigen after clearing out a bat-infested attic. Ten days after onset her fever is resolving and her cough is improving, and she is not short of breath. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Itraconazole for 12 months","Amphotericin-based induction","Fluconazole for 6 to 12 months","Observation without an antifungal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severity and host risk guide treatment, and mild acute disease in an immunocompetent adult often needs observation rather than antifungals. She is healthy and already improving, so neither itraconazole nor amphotericin is needed. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man ventilated for 6 days in the intensive care unit after cardiac surgery develops ventilator-associated pneumonia. Which organisms are most likely responsible?","choices":["Histoplasma or other endemic fungi","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Pseudomonas or multidrug-resistant organisms","Mycoplasma pneumoniae or Chlamydophila"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ventilator-associated pneumonia is more likely to be Pseudomonas or multidrug-resistant pathogens, and hospital and ventilator pneumonia are driven by Staphylococcus aureus and gram-negative bacilli, including Pseudomonas and Enterobacterales. It carries higher mortality and a longer intensive care stay. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 39, 48"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has had fever, sore throat and cough since yesterday. A single home antigen test for coronavirus disease 2019 is negative. What is the most appropriate next step in testing?","choices":["Repeat the antigen test after 48 hours","Accept that infection is excluded","Chest imaging to find the virus","A D-dimer level to confirm it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One negative antigen test does not exclude infection. A symptomatic person repeats the test after 48 hours for at least 2 tests, or obtains a nucleic acid amplification test, the most sensitive routine method. Her symptoms make the single negative result unreliable. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has 3 days of fever to 102.6°F, shaking chills, a cough with purulent sputum and a stabbing right-sided chest pain on breathing. His respiratory rate is 28/min. Over the right lower lobe there are crackles and dullness to percussion. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute bronchitis","Aspiration pneumonitis","Lung abscess","Community-acquired pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High fever, chills, purulent productive cough, pleuritic chest pain and tachypnea are more severe than a cold or bronchitis, and the findings sit over one lobe: crackles and dullness to percussion. Acquired outside the hospital, this is community-acquired pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 23, 27, 28, 50, 51, 53"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with a large lung abscess has not improved after several weeks of beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor therapy, and computed tomography shows no obstructing lesion. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Pulmonary or infectious disease review","Physical therapy for chest physiotherapy","Outpatient follow-up in 6 months","Switch to clindamycin, no referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Large or refractory abscesses need pulmonary or infectious disease review for drainage or surgery. Computed tomography and bronchoscopy are used for obstruction, unusual pathogens or failure to improve, and her abscess is both large and not improving. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with human immunodeficiency virus infection and chronic kidney disease is being treated with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for Pneumocystis pneumonia. Which tests should be monitored during treatment?","choices":["Blood count, creatinine, potassium","Lactate dehydrogenase trend","Weekly chest radiographs","Beta-D-glucan trend"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. During Pneumocystis treatment the complete blood count, creatinine, potassium and drug toxicity are monitored, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is adjusted for kidney function. Her chronic kidney disease makes the creatinine and potassium checks especially important. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia is newly disoriented to place and time. His blood urea nitrogen is 31 mg/dL, respiratory rate 32/min and blood pressure 86/52 mm Hg. What is his CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score?","choices":["2","3","4","5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Confusion 1 (new disorientation) + urea 1 (31 is above 20) + respiratory rate 1 (32 is 30 or more) + blood pressure 1 (systolic below 90) + age 0 (58) = 4. That is high severity, with a risk of death of 15 to 40%: hospital, urgent senior review and a decision about critical care, especially at 4 or 5. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman received a respiratory syncytial virus vaccine last year and asks whether she needs another dose this fall. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["Revaccinate every year","A second dose this fall","Revaccinate every 10 years","No repeat dose is recommended"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory syncytial virus vaccination is one dose for all adults aged 75 or older and for adults 50 to 74 at increased risk, and repeat annual dosing is not currently recommended. She already had her one dose. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 7, 124"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with acute bronchitis was managed with supportive care 10 days ago and at first improved. She now returns with a new temperature of 102.2°F, shortness of breath and crackles at the left base. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Chest radiograph for pneumonia","Empiric bronchitis antibiotic","Codeine cough syrup","Reassurance about cough length"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Worsening after initial improvement, fever and dyspnea are return precautions, and fever or focal findings prompt evaluation for pneumonia rather than empiric bronchitis antibiotics. A worsening cough means reconsidering the diagnosis, and suspected pneumonia is confirmed with chest radiography. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 18, 19, 20, 29"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia treated as an outpatient is afebrile, eating and clinically stable on day 3. What is the shortest total antibiotic course that may be appropriate for her?","choices":["3 days","5 days","7 days","10 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For clinically stable, nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia, a course shorter than 5 days, with a minimum of 3 days, may be appropriate. She is stable by day 3. Severe pneumonia is different: at least 5 days and until clinical stability. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 45, 47"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has influenza with a temperature of 102.8°F and body aches. His parent wants to give him a fever medicine from the cabinet at home. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["Acetaminophen","Ibuprofen","Oral fluids and rest","Aspirin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is avoided in children younger than 18 years with influenza because it is associated with Reye syndrome. Acetaminophen or ibuprofen is used for fever as part of supportive care, along with rest and fluids. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with alcohol use disorder has had 5 weeks of cough with foul-smelling sputum, intermittent hemoptysis, sweats and fatigue. Over the right upper chest there are amphoric breath sounds (a low-pitched sound with high overtones, like breathing across an open jar). Which chest imaging finding is expected?","choices":["Diffuse bilateral perihilar infiltrates","Homogeneous lobar consolidation","Diffuse fine reticular opacity","Cavity with an air-fluid level"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Weeks of fever-type symptoms, foul purulent sputum, hemoptysis as the cavity grows, and amphoric breath sounds over a large open cavity describe a lung abscess. Its chest radiograph and computed tomography show a cavitary lesion with an air-fluid level. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 33, 53, 54"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted to the intensive care unit with severe community-acquired pneumonia. Pseudomonas grew from his sputum during an admission last year. Which agent provides reliable antipseudomonal coverage?","choices":["Ertapenem","Vancomycin","Cefepime","Moxifloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prior respiratory isolation of Pseudomonas is assessed as a resistant-pathogen risk, so an active antipseudomonal agent is added. An antipseudomonal beta-lactam such as cefepime or piperacillin-tazobactam is used; moxifloxacin and ertapenem are not reliable options for Pseudomonas. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 40, 42, 25"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman hospitalized with coronavirus disease 2019 and hypoxemia has an elevated D-dimer. She has no signs of venous thrombosis and no bleeding risk. Which anticoagulation plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Hold anticoagulants until imaging","Prophylactic-dose anticoagulation","Therapeutic-dose anticoagulation","No anticoagulation for now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized patients receive venous thromboembolism prophylaxis unless contraindicated. Therapeutic dosing requires a thrombosis and bleeding assessment, and an elevated D-dimer alone neither diagnoses thrombosis nor sets the dose. She has no thrombosis, so the prophylactic dose fits. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 117, 121"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with no medical conditions has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. She has no risk for resistant organisms, and local pneumococcal resistance to macrolides is 30%. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Azithromycin as a single drug","Levofloxacin","Cefpodoxime plus azithromycin","Amoxicillin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With no comorbidities and no resistant-organism risk, the outpatient choices are amoxicillin or doxycycline. Macrolide monotherapy is used only if local pneumococcal resistance is below 25%, and hers is 30%, so amoxicillin fits. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man who had a kidney transplant takes prednisone. He has a cough and a new lung nodule, and his serum cryptococcal antigen is positive. Over the past 2 days he has developed a headache and new confusion. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Fluconazole and observe","Repeat chest film in 6 weeks","Urine Histoplasma antigen","Lumbar puncture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cryptococcosis is evaluated for central nervous system and disseminated disease, often with lumbar puncture. His transplant and steroid use raise his risk, and a new headache with confusion signals possible central nervous system spread, which changes him to amphotericin-based induction. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has had a sudden fever, myalgias, a frontal headache and a dry cough for one day, at the peak of a busy influenza season. A rapid influenza antigen test is negative. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["It does not exclude influenza","It rules out influenza","It points to a bacterial cause","She is no longer contagious"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid antigen tests have limited sensitivity, and a negative result does not exclude influenza, especially during high community activity. Her sudden typical illness at the peak of the season keeps influenza likely; molecular testing confirms it when the result affects management. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 42-year-old poultry farmer has fever, a nonproductive cough, severe headache and a unilateral lower-lobe infiltrate. Psittacosis is suspected. Which test is most useful for timely confirmation?","choices":["A single antibody titer","Blood count and liver enzymes","Respiratory nucleic acid test","Routine sputum culture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A respiratory nucleic acid amplification test, such as polymerase chain reaction, is timely and sensitive for Chlamydia psittaci, coordinated with public health laboratories. His poultry exposure and the lower-lobe infiltrate make that the confirming test. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 85"}]};
